theory.intro:
A formal proof of similarity names the two triangles with vertices in matching order. Most proofs use the equiangular test: give two pairs of equal angles, each with a reason. Matching sides of similar triangles are then in the same ratio.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.2,0);
\coordinate (C) at (0.8,2.0);
\coordinate (X) at (3.4,0);
\coordinate (Y) at (6.7,0);
\coordinate (Z) at (4.6,3.0);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw (X)--(Y)--(Z)--cycle;
\pic[draw, "$\alpha$", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle=B--A--C};
\pic[draw, "$\beta$", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle=C--B--A};
\pic[draw, "$\alpha$", angle radius=0.60cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle=Y--X--Z};
\pic[draw, "$\beta$", angle radius=0.60cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle=Z--Y--X};
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above left] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below left] at (X) {$X$};
\node[below right] at (Y) {$Y$};
\node[above] at (Z) {$Z$};
\node at (3.35,-1.3) {$\triangle ABC \sim \triangle XYZ$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

